{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-a1fd2e7d-b10f-4551-a18d-4e7c5fa6281b","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-8ff6f1a4a7fcde6b","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-9005-_i7jam9k/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-9005-E001.md","content":"# Validate gang admission semantics for multi-node training jobs at target scale\n\n## Description\n\nThis investigation resolves the strategy's primary open question: whether Kueue's current gang-admission semantics (waitForPodsReady) actually satisfy the all-or-nothing admission requirement for PyTorchJob and RayJob workloads at the platform's target scale, or whether scheduler-plugin work (e.g., a coscheduling plugin) beyond Kueue is required. The answer determines the shape of most of the implementation work in this strategy, so it must be settled before the admission, quota, and scheduling work is committed to an approach.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Reproduce the failure modes described in the problem statement at representative scale: multi-node training jobs (up to 16-GPU-class jobs) holding a partial allocation of GPUs for an extended period while waiting for remaining pods, and partially scheduled jobs dying on timeout.\n- Exercise Kueue's waitForPodsReady admission path with PyTorchJob and RayJob workloads at target scale, measuring time-to-full-allocation, partial-occupation windows, and timeout behavior under queue contention.\n- Identify failure modes of the native semantics: pods admitted piecemeal, partial GPU occupation while waiting for remaining pods, jobs lost to timeout after partial scheduling.\n- Evaluate the alternative: what scheduler-plugin work (e.g., coscheduling) would be required beyond Kueue, its maturity, and its interaction with Kueue admission and quota.\n- Deliverable: a decision record selecting the Kueue-native path or the scheduler-plugin path, with the measurement evidence, failure-mode analysis, and the implications for admission-time quota enforcement.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Measured evidence exists for PyTorchJob and RayJob gang admission at target scale, including partial-occupation windows and timeout behavior under contention, sufficient to reproduce or rule out the piecemeal-admission failure modes from the problem statement.\n- A decision record is produced that selects one of the two bounded outcomes — Kueue-native semantics sufficient (possibly with tuning), or scheduler-plugin work required — with supporting data and explicit pass/fail criteria against the all-or-nothing requirement (no partial GPU occupation while waiting for remaining pods).\n- The decision record states the implications for admission-time per-project quota enforcement so downstream implementation can proceed without re-litigating the approach.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-1 (P0): Multi-node training jobs are admitted all-or-nothing. This investigation determines whether the existing queueing layer's gang-admission semantics satisfy HLR-1 at target scale or whether scheduler-plugin work is required, and gates all HLR-1 implementation work.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":45504,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-89be6101d3983008","agent_id":"a87ab15cfd5e9f59c","session_id":"f72e5a6b-50db-4e86-b979-c3cde993f59c","uuid":"f344416f-6aeb-4ab3-8036-b37009bbc54f","timestamp":"2026-10-22T16:22:04.895Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-9005"}
