{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-88d279c9-6141-484f-b6bd-35929e4734b5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-b00f8ace1e675660","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-9005-_i7jam9k/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-9005-E002.md","content":"# Determine required preemption semantics for training workloads\n\n## Description\n\nThis investigation resolves the strategy's open question on preemption semantics: whether reference customers need full eviction with requeue, or checkpoint-aware graceful preemption. Field signals are conflicting and there is no benchmark of checkpoint overhead, so the answer requires both stakeholder input and measurement. The outcome selects between two structurally different implementations of the admin-defined preemption policy.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Elicit preemption requirements from reference customers running mixed training fleets: tolerance for full eviction with requeue versus expectation of checkpoint-aware graceful preemption.\n- Benchmark checkpoint overhead for representative distributed training jobs (checkpoint frequency, size, and interruption cost) to ground the trade-off between the two semantics.\n- Determine what the queueing layer can support for each option: eviction with requeue under an admin-defined policy, and graceful preemption coordinated with job checkpoint/restart.\n- Determine what the training-operator side must provide for checkpoint-aware graceful preemption (PyTorchJob checkpoint and restart-from-checkpoint behavior).\n- Deliverable: a decision record selecting the preemption semantics, the checkpoint-overhead benchmark results, and the requirements the selected semantics impose on the queueing layer and the training operator.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- A benchmark of checkpoint overhead for representative training jobs exists, covering checkpoint size, duration, and interruption cost, sufficient to compare full-eviction-with-requeue against checkpoint-aware graceful preemption.\n- A decision record selects one of the two bounded outcomes — full eviction with requeue, or checkpoint-aware graceful preemption — citing the elicited customer requirements and the benchmark data, with explicit rationale.\n- The decision record states the requirements the selected semantics impose on each affected component so downstream implementation can proceed without re-opening the semantics question.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-4 (P1): Higher-priority workloads can preempt lower-priority ones according to an admin-defined policy. This investigation determines which preemption semantics the implementation must provide, and gates all HLR-4 implementation work.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":46248,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-89be6101d3983008","agent_id":"a87ab15cfd5e9f59c","session_id":"f72e5a6b-50db-4e86-b979-c3cde993f59c","uuid":"ae238228-5aa4-4625-9829-36a8dfaf66bc","timestamp":"2026-10-22T16:22:19.138Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-9005"}
