{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-e933445b-2d19-4420-b611-6d065e7a0240","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-9e59695e81cf8178","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-9005-_i7jam9k/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-9005-BRANCH-B-E008.md","content":"# Checkpoint-aware graceful preemption in the queueing layer\n\n## Description\n\nThis branch epic implements checkpoint-aware graceful preemption in the queueing layer, selected when the preemption-semantics investigation determines that reference customers need graceful preemption rather than full eviction. Higher-priority workloads still preempt lower-priority ones per the admin-defined policy, but a preempted training job checkpoints its state and is requeued to resume from that checkpoint, informed by the checkpoint-overhead benchmark from the investigation.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Extend the queueing layer's preemption flow to coordinate graceful preemption: signal the workload to checkpoint before eviction, allow a bounded grace period consistent with the benchmarked checkpoint overhead, then evict and requeue.\n- Requeue preempted workloads so they resume from their checkpoint rather than restarting from scratch, under the admin-defined preemption policy.\n- Apply graceful preemption to PyTorchJob and RayJob workloads admitted through the queueing layer; the PyTorchJob checkpoint/restart support itself is separate work in the training-operator component within this same outcome.\n- Provide admin configuration for the preemption policy including grace-period bounds and fallback to full eviction when a workload cannot checkpoint.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- A higher-priority workload preempts a lower-priority one per the admin-defined policy, and the preempted job is given the opportunity to checkpoint within the configured grace period before eviction.\n- A preempted, requeued job that checkpointed successfully resumes from its checkpoint when re-admitted (not from scratch), for workloads whose job type supports checkpoint/restart.\n- A workload that fails to checkpoint within the grace period is fully evicted and requeued (fallback semantics), without leaking pods or GPU allocations.\n- Admins can define the preemption policy and grace-period bounds, and changes take effect on subsequent preemption decisions.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-4 (P1): Higher-priority workloads can preempt lower-priority ones according to an admin-defined policy — primary coverage for the queueing-layer side of the checkpoint-aware semantics selected by the preemption investigation.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":51652,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-89be6101d3983008","agent_id":"a87ab15cfd5e9f59c","session_id":"f72e5a6b-50db-4e86-b979-c3cde993f59c","uuid":"50f056d2-de7a-455c-97aa-5c07af200425","timestamp":"2026-10-22T16:24:18.473Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-9005"}
