{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-08cceadf-cddb-48da-a96b-71810741ccc6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-9981691a023d38c7","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-9005-_i7jam9k/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-9005-BRANCH-B-E009.md","content":"# PyTorchJob checkpoint/restart support for graceful preemption\n\n## Description\n\nThis branch epic delivers PyTorchJob checkpoint and restart-from-checkpoint support in the training operator, required by the checkpoint-aware graceful preemption path selected when the preemption-semantics investigation determines customers need graceful preemption. When a PyTorchJob is gracefully preempted, its state is checkpointed; when requeued and re-admitted, it resumes from that checkpoint. It builds against the graceful-preemption coordination provided by the queueing layer in this same outcome.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Support checkpoint initiation for PyTorchJob workloads on the graceful-preemption signal from the queueing layer: the job's training state is saved within the configured grace period.\n- Support restart-from-checkpoint when a preempted PyTorchJob is re-admitted, resuming training from the last checkpoint rather than from scratch.\n- Cover multi-node PyTorchJob topologies at representative GPU sizes.\n- Define checkpoint storage/retention requirements for preempted jobs (where checkpoints live and how long they are kept for resume), consistent with the checkpoint-overhead benchmark from the preemption investigation.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- A gracefully preempted PyTorchJob produces a usable checkpoint within the grace period, including multi-node topologies.\n- A re-admitted PyTorchJob resumes training from its checkpoint (verified by resumed-step/loss continuity versus a from-scratch run), including multi-node topologies.\n- Checkpoint storage and retention behave per the defined requirements (preempted jobs' checkpoints are available for resume and expire per retention policy).\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-4 (P1): Higher-priority workloads can preempt lower-priority ones according to an admin-defined policy — provides the PyTorchJob checkpoint/restart capability the checkpoint-aware preemption semantics depend on.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":52278,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-89be6101d3983008","agent_id":"a87ab15cfd5e9f59c","session_id":"f72e5a6b-50db-4e86-b979-c3cde993f59c","uuid":"6d3e3389-170f-4049-8170-9927876100ef","timestamp":"2026-10-22T16:24:33.561Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-9005"}
