{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9eaeb42ddb29587f","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9005\n3\tepic_count: 6\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\trevised: false\n7\ttriage: null\n8\ttriage_rationale: null\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9005\n11\t\n12\t**Strategy**: Queue-aware GPU scheduling for distributed training workloads\n13\t**Triage**: Full decomposition (not below-threshold: spans multiple components and teams, and contains three open questions whose answers change which implementation epics exist or what they do; not docs-only: code changes are required in Distributed Workloads, Training Operator, and Dashboard).\n14\t\n15\t## Epic List\n16\t\n17\tMain plan:\n18\t\n19\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n20\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n21\t| RHAISTRAT-9005-E001 | Validate gang admission semantics for multi-node training jobs at target scale | Investigation | Distributed Workloads | P0 |\n22\t| RHAISTRAT-9005-E002 | Determine required preemption semantics for training workloads | Investigation | Distributed Workloads | P1 |\n23\t| RHAISTRAT-9005-E003 | Assess maturity of node topology discovery for topology-aware placement | Investigation | Distributed Workloads | P2 |\n24\t| RHAISTRAT-9005-E004 | Per-project GPU quota enforcement at admission for training job types | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P0 |\n25\t| RHAISTRAT-9005-E005 | Pending-job queue visibility in the Dashboard | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n26\t| RHAISTRAT-9005-E006 | Topology-aware GPU placement for training workloads | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P2 |\n27\t\n28\tConditional branch epics (attached as plan documents to their gating Investigation, not standalone Jira issues):\n29\t\n30\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority | Gate |\n31\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|------|\n32\t| RHAISTRAT-9005-BRANCH-A-E007 | Kueue-native gang admission hardening for training jobs | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P0 | E001 outcome A |\n33\t| RHAISTRAT-9005-BRANCH-B-E007 | Coscheduling scheduler-plugin enablement for gang admission | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P0 | E001 outcome B |\n34\t| RHAISTRAT-9005-BRANCH-A-E008 | Admin-defined preemption policy with eviction and requeue | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P1 | E002 outcome A |\n35\t| RHAISTRAT-9005-BRANCH-B-E008 | Checkpoint-aware graceful preemption in the queueing layer | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P1 | E002 outcome B |\n36\t| RHAISTRAT-9005-BRANCH-B-E009 | PyTorchJob checkpoint/restart support for graceful preemption | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P1 | E002 outcome B |\n37\t\n38\t## Dependency DAG\n39\t\n40\t```mermaid\n41\tgraph TD\n42\t    E001[\"E001 Gang admission investigation (P0)\"]\n43\t    E002[\"E002 Preemption semantics investigation (P1)\"]\n44\t    E003[\"E003 Topology maturity investigation (P2)\"]\n45\t    E004[\"E004 Per-project GPU quota (P0)\"]\n46\t    E005[\"E005 Dashboard queue visibility (P1)\"]\n47\t    E006[\"E006 Topology-aware placement (P2)\"]\n48\t    AE007[\"BRANCH-A-E007 Kueue-native gang admission (P0)\"]\n49\t    BE007[\"BRANCH-B-E007 Coscheduling plugin (P0)\"]\n50\t    AE008[\"BRANCH-A-E008 Eviction+requeue preemption (P1)\"]\n51\t    BE008[\"BRANCH-B-E008 Graceful preemption, queueing layer (P1)\"]\n52\t    BE009[\"BRANCH-B-E009 PyTorchJob checkpoint support (P1)\"]\n53\t\n54\t    E001 --> E004\n55\t    E001 --> AE007\n56\t    E001 --> BE007\n57\t    E002 --> AE008\n58\t    E002 --> BE008\n59\t    BE008 --> BE009\n60\t    E003 --> E006\n61\t    E004 --> E005\n62\t```\n63\t\n64\tArrows point from dependency to dependent (`X --> Y` = X must complete before Y starts).\n65\t\n66\t## DAG Justification\n67\t\n68\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n69\t|------|------|-----------|\n70\t| E001 → E004 | Rule 3 (Investigation edges) | Whether Kueue-native admission suffices or scheduler-plugin work is needed changes the shape of admission-time enforcement, including where per-project quota is hooked. True gate: `rewrite` — quota enforcement exists in either outcome but its approach changes. |\n71\t| E001 → BRANCH-A-E007 | Rule 3, bounded outcomes (2) | Outcome A (Kueue waitForPodsReady sufficient): harden/tune the native path. Branch epic removed if outcome A does not hold. |\n72\t| E001 → BRANCH-B-E007 | Rule 3, bounded outcomes (2) | Outcome B (scheduler-plugin work required): enable a coscheduling scheduler plugin — work that only exists in this outcome. |\n73\t| E002 → BRANCH-A-E008 | Rule 3, bounded outcomes (2) | Outcome A (full eviction with requeue is sufficient): configure Kueue preemption under an admin-defined policy. |\n74\t| E002 → BRANCH-B-E008 | Rule 3, bounded outcomes (2) | Outcome B (checkpoint-aware graceful preemption required): different implementation in the same component. |\n75\t| BRANCH-B-E008 → BRANCH-B-E009 | Rule 1 / Rule 13 | Checkpoint-aware graceful preemption spans two components: the queueing layer coordinates the preemption; PyTorchJob checkpoint/restart support builds against that coordination surface. Different canonical components → separate branch epics inside branch B. |\n76\t| E003 → E006 | Rule 3 | Topology-aware placement exists only if node topology discovery (Tech Preview) is mature enough this release. True gate: `remove` — the epic may not exist. |\n77\t| E004 → E005 | Rule 13 | The Dashboard builds against the per-project quota/admission model that the quota epic establishes (per-project queue and quota-consumption surfaces it displays). |\n78\t| (no edge) E004 ∥ BRANCH-*-E007 | Rule 15 | Quota configuration and gang-admission semantics touch the same repos but different areas of the admission configuration; coordination risk, not a dependency. |\n79\t| (no edge) E001 ∥ E002 ∥ E003 | Rule 14 | Independent investigations with no shared artifacts; run in parallel. |\n80\t| (no edge) Dashboard ∥ gang-admission branches | Rule 14 | The Dashboard reads queue position and admission state from existing Kueue workload/metrics interfaces that exist in either admission outcome; no branch epic is referenced by a main-plan epic (branch rule 2). |\n81\t\n82\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n83\t\n84\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Coverage |\n85\t|-----|----------|---------|----------|\n86\t| HLR-1 | P0 | E001 (investigation); BRANCH-A-E007 or BRANCH-B-E007 (implementation, selected by E001 outcome) | All-or-nothing admission for multi-node training jobs. |\n87\t| HLR-2 | P0 | E004 | Per-project GPU quota enforced at admission across PyTorchJob and RayJob. |\n88\t| HLR-3 | P1 | E005 | Queue position, quota consumption, and admission state visible in the Dashboard. |\n89\t| HLR-4 | P1 | E002 (investigation); BRANCH-A-E008 or BRANCH-B-E008 + BRANCH-B-E009 (implementation, selected by E002 outcome) | Admin-defined preemption policy; semantics determined by E002. |\n90\t| HLR-5 | P2 | E003 (investigation); E006 (implementation, gated by E003) | Topology-aware placement, contingent on topology discovery maturity. |\n91\t\n92\tAll five HLRs are covered; none orphaned.\n93\t\n94\t## Component Mapping Notes\n95\t\n96\t- All three strategy components (`Distributed Workloads`, `Training Operator`, `Dashboard`) exist verbatim in `.context/rhai-components.txt` and in `.context/architecture-context/` — no new-component onboarding chain is needed; all are Konflux-onboarded.\n97\t- **Training Operator is passive for HLR-1/HLR-2**: per architecture context, the operator performs no scheduling — PyTorchJob admission flows through Kueue integration, and both Training Operator and Distributed Workloads are owned by the same team (Distributed Workloads). Validation that PyTorchJob workloads are admitted all-or-nothing and counted against project quota is therefore captured as acceptance criteria on the Distributed Workloads epics (E004 and the gang-admission branch epics), not as separate epics.\n98\t- **Training Operator is active only for checkpoint-aware graceful preemption** (BRANCH-B-E009), where checkpoint/restart support requires operator/framework changes.\n99\t- **RayJob/KubeRay work** falls under the canonical `Distributed Workloads` component (kuberay is a Distributed Workloads midstream per architecture context); no separate canonical component exists for it.\n100\t- **Coscheduling scheduler plugin (BRANCH-B-E007)** is not a shipped platform component (architecture context states Volcano is not shipped). It is assigned the canonical `Distributed Workloads` component as the closest match; the span is noted here.\n101\t\n102\t## Investigation Decisions\n103\t\n104\t| Open question (from strategy) | Changes downstream epics? | Handling |\n105\t|-------------------------------|---------------------------|----------|\n106\t| OQ-1: Do Kueue's gang-admission semantics (waitForPodsReady) satisfy HLR-1 at target scale, or is scheduler-plugin work needed? | Yes — \"the answer changes the shape of most implementation work\" | Investigation E001 with two bounded outcomes → conditional branches A/B for the HLR-1 implementation. |\n107\t| OQ-2: Is Tech Preview node topology discovery mature enough to build HLR-5 on this release? | Yes — HLR-5 implementation may not exist | Investigation E003; topology-aware placement (E006) is a true gate with `remove` action. Bounded outcomes, but the \"not mature\" outcome produces no work, so a single gated main-plan epic expresses it more accurately than an empty branch. |\n108\t| OQ-3: What preemption semantics do reference customers need — full eviction with requeue, or checkpoint-aware graceful? | Yes — different implementations, and outcome B spans an additional component | Investigation E002 (includes the missing checkpoint-overhead benchmark) with two bounded outcomes → conditional branches A/B. |\n109\t\n110\t## Health Warnings\n111\t\n112\t- **Missing strategy sections**: the strategy has no Impacted Teams table, no Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then Acceptance Criteria, no Non-Functional Requirements, no Technical Approach, and no Staff Engineer Input. Owner teams were inferred from architecture context (Distributed Workloads owns both Distributed Workloads and Training Operator; RHOAI Dashboard owns Dashboard). Epic acceptance criteria were derived from the HLRs and problem statement rather than strategy-level ACs.\n113\t- **Scope trap (HLR-5)**: building P2 topology-aware placement on a Tech Preview feature risks rework when the discovery API changes at GA. Mitigated by gating E006 on the maturity investigation E003 (`remove` if not ready).\n114\t- **Priority note**: preemption (HLR-4, P1) carries unresolved product semantics with conflicting field signals; implementation is correctly blocked behind investigation E002, and checkpoint-aware graceful preemption adds cross-component work (Training Operator) that the P1 label may underweight.\n115\t- **Mixed-fleet topology throughput claims** (\"significant throughput loss\" from crossing NVLink domains) are unevidenced in the strategy; the topology investigation should capture representative measurements before committing to the P2 placement work.\n116\t- **Out-of-scope boundaries** (GPU sharing/fractioning, multi-cluster queueing) are respected — no epic touches MIG, time-slicing policy, or cross-cluster queueing.\n117\t\n118\t## Tiered Delivery\n119\t\n120\tNot applicable. No external dependency with uncertain timing: Kueue, training-operator, and kuberay all have midstream repos under opendatahub-io (no upstream-acceptance gate blocks delivery). The coscheduling scheduler plugin in branch B is a new platform dependency, but it is selected by the gang-admission investigation outcome, not by external timing.\n121\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-97427b6cdc973e21","agent_id":"a8f861f4d9cfa2f37","session_id":"f72e5a6b-50db-4e86-b979-c3cde993f59c","uuid":"5b01aadb-89a4-4f88-a45c-ac3f6651