{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-89e3b168a177cc38","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-9005-BRANCH-B-E008\n3\ttitle: Checkpoint-aware graceful preemption in the queueing layer\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9005\n5\tcomponent: Distributed Workloads\n6\tteam: Distributed Workloads\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P1\n9\tbranch: B\n10\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-9005-E002\n11\tdependencies:\n12\t- RHAISTRAT-9005-E002\n13\tgate_failure_impact:\n14\t  action: remove\n15\t  fallback_approach: If full eviction with requeue is sufficient, this graceful-preemption\n16\t    branch is dropped in favor of the eviction-with-requeue branch.\n17\tai_signals:\n18\t  change_specificity: 0\n19\t  pattern_precedent: 0\n20\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n21\t  existing_foundation: 1\n22\t  open_questions: 0\n23\t  external_dependency: -1\n24\t  human_process_gates: 0\n25\t  repo_access: 1\n26\t  architecture_claims: 1\n27\timplementation_type: null\n28\tinvestigation_signals: null\n29\tai_implementability: Medium\n30\tai_implementability_score: 2\n31\tjira_key: null\n32\t---\n33\t# Checkpoint-aware graceful preemption in the queueing layer\n34\t\n35\t## Description\n36\t\n37\tThis branch epic implements checkpoint-aware graceful preemption in the queueing layer, selected when the preemption-semantics investigation determines that reference customers need graceful preemption rather than full eviction. Higher-priority workloads still preempt lower-priority ones per the admin-defined policy, but a preempted training job checkpoints its state and is requeued to resume from that checkpoint, informed by the checkpoint-overhead benchmark from the investigation.\n38\t\n39\t## Scope\n40\t\n41\t- Extend the queueing layer's preemption flow to coordinate graceful preemption: signal the workload to checkpoint before eviction, allow a bounded grace period consistent with the benchmarked checkpoint overhead, then evict and requeue.\n42\t- Requeue preempted workloads so they resume from their checkpoint rather than restarting from scratch, under the admin-defined preemption policy.\n43\t- Apply graceful preemption to PyTorchJob and RayJob workloads admitted through the queueing layer; the PyTorchJob checkpoint/restart support itself is separate work in the training-operator component within this same outcome.\n44\t- Provide admin configuration for the preemption policy including grace-period bounds and fallback to full eviction when a workload cannot checkpoint.\n45\t\n46\t## Acceptance Criteria\n47\t\n48\t- A higher-priority workload preempts a lower-priority one per the admin-defined policy, and the preempted job is given the opportunity to checkpoint within the configured grace period before eviction.\n49\t- A preempted, requeued job that checkpointed successfully resumes from its checkpoint when re-admitted (not from scratch), for workloads whose job type supports checkpoint/restart.\n50\t- A workload that fails to checkpoint within the grace period is fully evicted and requeued (fallback semantics), without leaking pods or GPU allocations.\n51\t- Admins can define the preemption policy and grace-period bounds, and changes take effect on subsequent preemption decisions.\n52\t\n53\t## HLR Traceability\n54\t\n55\t- HLR-4 (P1): Higher-priority workloads can preempt lower-priority ones according to an admin-defined policy — primary coverage for the queueing-layer side of the checkpoint-aware semantics selected by the preemption investigation.\n56\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-97427b6cdc973e21","agent_id":"a8f861f4d9cfa2f37","session_id":"f72e5a6b-50db-4e86-b979-c3cde993f59c","uuid":"10008e7f-21ad-44bd-9d3e-27227d1d838b","timestamp":"2026-10-22T16:29:48.087Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-89e3b168a177cc38","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-9005 decomposition"