gth 3 (E001 → E004 → E005, and E001 → BE008 → BE009 within\n  branch B) is within the M-standard heuristic (3-4) for a 3-component, 5-HLR\n  strategy.\n\nContent scan for uncaptured data dependencies: none found — E006's placement\npreference composes with existing admission/quota rules rather than consuming an\nartifact from those epics, and E005's queue-position/admission-state reads come\nfrom pre-existing interfaces (explicitly justified in the DAG notes).\n\n### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n\n- Component/team tuples are respected: all Distributed Workloads epics are\n  component=Distributed Workloads; E005 is Dashboard/RHOAI Dashboard; BRANCH-B-E009\n  is component=Training Operator, correctly split from BRANCH-B-E008 (queueing\n  layer) per the different-component rule even though the same team owns both.\n- No epic bundles multiple components or teams. Training Operator's passivity for\n  HLR-1/HLR-2 is handled as acceptance criteria on the owning team's epics (E004\n  validates PyTorchJob admission through the queueing layer) rather than as a\n  forced separate epic — the right call given single-team ownership and no\n  operator code change.\n- No scope duplication across siblings: the A/B branch pairs are mutually\n  exclusive outcomes of the same gate, not parallel builds of the same surface;\n  no two epics independently build the same data structure or UI surface.\n- No work-product type mixing (no content authoring bundled with code).\n- Epic sizes are plausible single-sprint units; nothing approaches multi-sprint\n  size. The coscheduling plugin epic (BE007) is the largest but is an\n  enablement/integration epic, not greenfield scheduler development.\n\n### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n\n- All three Investigations genuinely resolve uncertainty that changes downstream\n  structure: E001 selects between two admission implementations (and rewrites\n  E004's hook point), E002 selects between two preemption implementations plus\n  an extra component's work, E003 decides whether E006 exists at all.\n- All Implementation epics produce concrete artifacts (quota model + config\n  surface, Dashboard views, admission configuration, scheduler plugin\n  integration, preemption policy, checkpoint/restart support).\n- Gating metadata is complete and correct: every epic with `gated_by` has\n  `gate_failure_impact` with both `action` and `fallback_approach` populated;\n  every epic listing an Investigation in `dependencies` has `gated_by` set;\n  every `gated_by` target is in the direct `dependencies` list. E004's\n  `rewrite` action correctly describes a real scope change (quota hook point\n  moves) rather than a mere scheduling dependency.\n\n### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n\nSignal-type hygiene is correct: Investigations carry `investigation_signals`\n(5-signal set), Implementations carry `ai_signals` (9-signal set); no mixing, and\nall eleven ai-signals rationale files exist with per-signal rationales matching\nthe frontmatter values.\n\nSpot-checked values against strategy content:\n\n- E001 `cluster_hardware_dependence: -2` is right — the gating question is\n  explicitly about behavior \"at our target scale\" for 16-GPU-class jobs.\n- E002 `human_judgment_required: -2` and `source_accessibility: 0` match the\n  strategy's \"conflicting field signals\" wording — the answer lives in\n  stakeholder input, not a readable artifact.\n- E003 `human_judgment_required: -1` correctly captures the go/no-go risk call\n  on top of desk research.\n- E006 `open_questions: -1` (existence gated on an unresolved investigation)\n  and `pattern_precedent: 0` / `existing_foundation: 0` (Tech Preview only)\n  are consistent with the strategy's Tech Preview caveat.\n- BRANCH-B-E007 `pattern_precedent: -1` (no gang scheduler shipped) with\n  `adapter_pattern: 1` (upstream scheduler-plugins recipe) is a well-reasoned\n  distinction, as is BRANCH-B-E008 `external_dependency: -1` (graceful\n  preemption hooks likely requiring upstream contribution).\n- E005 `open_questions: 1` is justified: the Dashboard surface is identical\n  under either admission/preemption outcome.\n\nNo signal value contradicts the rubric conditions or the strategy content.\n\n### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n\n- Every epic has testable ACs derived from the HLRs and problem statement\n  (e.g., \"no partial GPU occupation while waiting for remaining pods, verified\n  at target scale under queue contention\"; \"a preempted, requeued job that\n  checkpointed successfully resumes from its checkpoint\").\n- Rollback/reversibility ACs are present on both admission-behavior epics\n  (BRANCH-A-E007: reverting admission settings restores prior behavior;\n  BRANCH-B-E007: disabling the plugin restores default scheduling) — these are\n  the replacement-behavior epics in this plan.\n- No rule-mandated AC is missing: no konflux-chain epics and no docs-authoring\n  epic exist (the strategy has no Prerequisites & Process Gates section, so no\n  documentation-support requirement is being dropped).\n- No non-testable ACs: no external-schedule constraints (lab turnaround,\n  review-board cadence) are misfiled as ACs; timing risks live in the summary's\n  Health Warnings where they belong.\n- No draft-ID references (\"E001\" etc.) in any epic body — epics reference each\n  other by role (\"the gang-admission semantics investigation\", \"the\n  admission-time quota enforcement work\"), which survives Jira key assignment.\n\n### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n\n- All strategy scope is covered: all-or-nothing admission (HLR-1 via E001 +\n  branches), per-project quota (HLR-2 via E004), Dashboard visibility (HLR-3 via\n  E005), preemption (HLR-4 via E002 + branches), topology placement (HLR-5 via\n  E003 + E006). PyTorchJob and RayJob coverage is carried explicitly through\n  E004, E005, E006, and the branch epics' ACs.\n- Conditional branches cover all bounded outcomes: OQ-1 and OQ-3 each have\n  explicit A/B branches; OQ-2's \"not mature\" outcome produces no work, and the\n  single gated main-plan epic with `remove` action expresses that more\n  accurately than an empty branch — a defensible and documented choice.\n- Strategy-level context is carried forward, not dropped: all three open\n  questions become Investigations with decision-record deliverables; the\n  Tech Preview risk becomes E003's gate; the missing checkpoint-overhead\n  benchmark is folded into E002's scope; the unevidenced NVLink throughput\n  claims and the priority-underweighting concern are recorded in Health\n  Warnings; out-of-scope boundaries (MIG, time-slicing, multi-cluster) are\n  respected by every epic.\n- Cross-epic consistency: the summary's Component Mapping Notes account for\n  all three affected components, including the RayJob/kuberay attribution and\n  the coscheduling plugin's non-shipped status.\n\nOne observation, not scored: under branch B, RayJob workloads fall back to full\neviction when they cannot checkpoint (BRANCH-B-E008 AC 3 wording \"for workloads\nwhose job type supports checkpoint/restart\"). This is a reasonable reading of\nHLR-4 — which requires preemption, not checkpoint-resume for every job type —\nand the fallback semantics are explicitly testable, so it is not a gap.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":35975,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-97427b6cdc973e21","agent_id":"a8f861f4d9cfa2f37","session_id":"f72e5a6b-50db-4e86-b979-c3cde993f59c","uuid":"add661a7-58d3-4285-873d-6fc956970729","timestamp":"2026-10-22T16:32:50.867Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-9005 decomposition"}
