{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8fc3d373b598b3a9","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-9005\n3\tscore: 14\n4\tpass: true\n5\trecommendation: accept\n6\tissues: []\n7\terror: null\n8\t---\n9\t# Decomposition Review — RHAISTRAT-9005\n10\t\n11\t## Review Summary\n12\t\n13\tScore: 14/14 — pass\n14\tRecommendation: accept\n15\t\n16\tContract validator: OK (all mechanical checks passed: epic body sections, canonical\n17\tcomponent names, docs-last coverage, priority inheritance).\n18\t\n19\tTriage consistency: `triage: null` (full decomposition) with 6 main-plan epics plus\n20\tconditional branches — multi-epic output is expected; no contradiction.\n21\t\n22\tThe decomposition is a clean conditional-branch plan: three Investigations (one per\n23\tstrategy open question), a main-plan implementation set gated where outcomes change\n24\tthe work's shape, and A/B branch epics for the two genuinely binary open questions\n25\t(OQ-1 gang admission, OQ-3 preemption semantics). HLR traceability is complete and\n26\thonest about which HLRs are covered by investigations versus implementations.\n27\t\n28\t## Criterion Details\n29\t\n30\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n31\t\n32\tAll five HLRs map to at least one epic, and the traceability matrix in the\n33\tdecomposition summary is present and matches the epic files:\n34\t\n35\t- HLR-1 (P0): E001 investigation + BRANCH-A-E007 / BRANCH-B-E007 (outcome-selected).\n36\t- HLR-2 (P0): E004.\n37\t- HLR-3 (P1): E005.\n38\t- HLR-4 (P1): E002 investigation + BRANCH-A-E008 / BRANCH-B-E008 + BRANCH-B-E009.\n39\t- HLR-5 (P2): E003 investigation + E006 (gated, `remove` on failure — P2 work is\n40\t  implemented rather than deferred, and the gate expresses the deferral condition).\n41\t\n42\tPriority inheritance is correct in every case: E001 (P0) gates P0 work; E002 (P1)\n43\tgates P1 work; E003 (P2) gates P2 work; E004 (P0) → E005 (P1) runs downhill. No\n44\tlower-priority epic