flux/repo/license onboarding epics exist (all\n73\t  components are in the platform), so rules 7/8/10 pairings are vacuous.\n74\t- Critical path length 3 (E001 → E004 → E005, and E001 → BE008 → BE009 within\n75\t  branch B) is within the M-standard heuristic (3-4) for a 3-component, 5-HLR\n76\t  strategy.\n77\t\n78\tContent scan for uncaptured data dependencies: none found — E006's placement\n79\tpreference composes with existing admission/quota rules rather than consuming an\n80\tartifact from those epics, and E005's queue-position/admission-state reads come\n81\tfrom pre-existing interfaces (explicitly justified in the DAG notes).\n82\t\n83\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n84\t\n85\t- Component/team tuples are respected: all Distributed Workloads epics are\n86\t  component=Distributed Workloads; E005 is Dashboard/RHOAI Dashboard; BRANCH-B-E009\n87\t  is component=Training Operator, correctly split from BRANCH-B-E008 (queueing\n88\t  layer) per the different-component rule even though the same team owns both.\n89\t- No epic bundles multiple components or teams. Training Operator's passivity for\n90\t  HLR-1/HLR-2 is handled as acceptance criteria on the owning team's epics (E004\n91\t  validates PyTorchJob admission through the queueing layer) rather than as a\n92\t  forced separate epic — the right call given single-team ownership and no\n93\t  operator code change.\n94\t- No scope duplication across siblings: the A/B branch pairs are mutually\n95\t  exclusive outcomes of the same gate, not parallel builds of the same surface;\n96\t  no two epics independently build the same data structure or UI surface.\n97\t- No work-product type mixing (no content authoring bundled with code).\n98\t- Epic sizes are plausible single-sprint units; nothing approaches multi-sprint\n99\t  size. The coscheduling plugin epic (BE007) is the largest but is an\n100\t  enablement/integration epic, not greenfield scheduler development.\n101\t\n102\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n103\t\n104\t- All three Investigations genuinely resolve uncertainty that changes downstream\n105\t  structure: E001 selects between two admission implementations (and rewrites\n106\t  E004's hook point), E002 selects between two preemption implementations plus\n107\t  an extra component's work, E003 decides whether E006 exists at all.\n108\t- All Implementation epics produce concrete artifacts (quota model + config\n109\t  surface, Dashboard views, admission configuration, scheduler plugin\n110\t  integration, preemption policy, checkpoint/restart support).\n111\t- Gating metadata is complete and correct: every epic with `gated_by` has\n112\t  `gate_failure_impact` with both `action` and `fallback_approach` populated;\n113\t  every epic listing an Investigation in `dependencies` has `gated_by` set;\n114\t  every `gated_by` target is in the direct `dependencies` list. E004's\n115\t  `rewrite` action correctly describes a real scope change (quota hook point\n116\t  moves) rather than a mere scheduling dependency.\n117\t\n118\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n119\t\n120\tSignal-type hygiene is correct: Investigations carry `investigation_signals`\n121\t(5-signal set), Implementations carry `ai_signals` (9-signal set); no mixing, and\n122\tall eleven ai-signals rationale files exist with per-signal rationales matching\n123\tthe frontmatter values.\n124\t\n125\tSpot-checked values against strategy content:\n126\t\n127\t- E001 `cluster_hardware_dependence: -2` is right — the gating question is\n128\t  explicitly about behavior \"at our target scale\" for 16-GPU-class jobs.\n129\t- E002 `human_judgment_required: -2` and `source_accessibility: 0` match the\n130\t  strategy's \"conflicting field signals\" wording — the answer lives in\n131\t  stakeholder input, not a readable artifact.\n132\t- E003 `human_judgment_required: -1` correctly captures the go/no-go risk call\n133\t  on top of desk research.\n134\t- E006 `open_questions: -1` (existence gated on an unresolved investigation)\n135\t  and `pattern_precedent: 0` / `existing_foundation: 0` (Tech Preview only)\n136\t  are consistent with the strategy's Tech Preview caveat.\n137\t- BRANCH-B-E007 `pattern_precedent: -1` (no gang scheduler shipped) with\n138\t  `adapter_pattern: 1` (upstream scheduler-plugins recipe) is a well-reasoned\n139\t  distinction, as is BRANCH-B-E008 `external_dependency: -1` (graceful\n140\t  preemption hooks likely requiring upstream contribution).\n141\t- E005 `open_questions: 1` is justified: the Dashboard surface is identical\n142\t  under either admission/preemption outcome.\n143\t\n144\tNo signal value contradicts the rubric conditions or the strategy content.\n145\t\n146\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n147\t\n148\t- Every epic has testable ACs derived from the HLRs and problem statement\n149\t  (e.g., \"no partial GPU occupation while waiting for remaining pods, verified\n150\t  at target scale under queue contention\"; \"a preempted, requeued job that\n151\t  checkpointed successfully resumes from its checkpoint\").\n152\t- Rollback/reversibility ACs are present on both admission-behavior epics\n153\t  (BRANCH-A-E007: reverting admission settings restores prior behavior;\n154\t  BRANCH-B-E007: disabling the plugin restores default scheduling) — these are\n155\t  the replacement-behavior epics in this plan.\n156\t- No rule-mandated AC is missing: no konflux-chain epics and no docs-authoring\n157\t  epic exist (the strategy has no Prerequisites & Process Gates section, so no\n158\t  documentation-support requirement is being dropped).\n159\t- No non-testable ACs: no external-schedule constraints (lab turnaround,\n160\t  review-board cadence) are misfiled as ACs; timing risks live in the summary's\n161\t  Health Warnings where they belong.\n162\t- No draft-ID references (\"E001\" etc.) in any epic body — epics reference each\n163\t  other by role (\"the gang-admission semantics investigation\", \"the\n164\t  admission-time quota enforcement work